## Summary

The `array * int` operator bypasses `LoopLimit` whenever the left operand is a lazy sequence (`ScriptRange`) rather than a materialized array (`ScriptArray`). An attacker-supplied template such as `([1] | array.map 'x') * 2000000000 | array.size` drives billions of uncharged internal iterations, pinning a CPU core for minutes and thrashing the GC — even with `LoopLimit` set to `1`. This is the missed sibling of [GHSA-q6rr-fm2g-g5x8](https://github.com/scriban/scriban/security/advisories/GHSA-q6rr-fm2g-g5x8), whose 7.2.1 fix hardened only the `ScriptArray` multiplication path.

## Root Cause

`array * int` dispatches on the left operand's concrete type. A `[...]` literal is a `ScriptArray` and routes to `ScriptArray<T>.TryEvaluate`, which the 7.2.1 fix ([`205ca6a`](https://github.com/scriban/scriban/commit/205ca6a7c2349d3d388bd5f1f7729ee198c0d5e5)) hardened with a pre-check at [`ScriptArray.cs#L510-L519`](https://github.com/scriban/scriban/blob/c3f03bfc912e14b306a01a03e611f606b05f9c33/src/Scriban/Runtime/ScriptArray.cs#L510-L519):

```csharp
var resultLength = (long)intModifier * array.Count;
if (resultLength > int.MaxValue) { throw ... }
if (context.LoopLimit > 0 && resultLength > context.LoopLimit) { throw ... }  // bounded BEFORE the loop
```

But the return value of every lazy builtin — `array.map`, `array.filter`, `array.each`, `array.reverse`, `array.uniq`, `array.compact`, `array.concat`, `array.offset`, `array.limit` — is a `ScriptRange`, which routes `*` to [`ScriptRange.Multiply`](https://github.com/scriban/scriban/blob/c3f03bfc912e14b306a01a03e611f606b05f9c33/src/Scriban/Runtime/ScriptRange.cs#L648) → [`MultiplyImpl(context,...)` at `ScriptRange.cs#L368-L380`](https://github.com/scriban/scriban/blob/c3f03bfc912e14b306a01a03e611f606b05f9c33/src/Scriban/Runtime/ScriptRange.cs#L368-L380):

```csharp
int loopStep = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)          // outer repetition: bounded ONLY by attacker `count`
{
    foreach (var value in left)          // StepLoop charged HERE — per element emitted
    {
        context.StepLoop(span, ref loopStep, loopType);
        yield return value;
    }
}
```

The single `LoopLimit` charge lives inside the inner element loop. `StepLoop` increments a per-call local counter (`ref loopStep`), so when `left` enumerates to **zero** elements it is never called, yet the outer `for` still executes `count` times, re-enumerating `left` each time. `ScriptRange.Multiply` never received the `count`-vs-`LoopLimit` pre-check that fixed `ScriptArray.Multiply`. An empty lazy sequence is trivially produced — e.g. `array.map 'x'` over non-object elements yields nothing, as does `[] | array.reverse` or `array.offset <past-end>`.

`src/Scriban/Runtime/ScriptRange.cs` is byte-identical at tag `7.2.5` and `master` HEAD; there are no open pull requests. The documented contract is the opposite of the behaviour: [`safe-runtime.md`](https://github.com/scriban/scriban/blob/c3f03bfc912e14b306a01a03e611f606b05f9c33/site/docs/runtime/safe-runtime.md?plain=1#L75) states `LoopLimit` "limits internal iteration/allocation work such as range expressions (`1..100000`) and array multiplication."

