## Impact

Any application that renders attacker-controlled Scriban templates — the engine's documented use case (CMS, email/report templating, static-site generation, template-as-a-service) — can be driven to denial of service by a tiny template. A single request pins a CPU core and saturates the garbage collector for as long as the attacker's `count` dictates, entirely bypassing the `LoopLimit` safeguard operators rely on to sandbox untrusted templates (the bypass holds even at `LoopLimit = 1`). Same class and threat model as the prior accepted advisories GHSA-q6rr-fm2g-g5x8, [GHSA-24c8-4792-22hx](https://github.com/scriban/scriban/security/advisories/GHSA-24c8-4792-22hx), and [GHSA-c875-h985-hvrc](https://github.com/scriban/scriban/security/advisories/GHSA-c875-h985-hvrc). Availability only — no confidentiality or integrity impact.

## Summary
The `ActivityMonitor` Livewire component exposes a public `$activityId` property without Livewire's `#[Locked]` attribute. It loads activities via `Activity::find($this->activityId)` with no authorization or team scoping. Activity IDs are auto-incrementing integers. Any authenticated user can enumerate activity records across all teams and read the full command output from remote SSH processes, which may include secrets, configuration files, and infrastructure details.

## Severity
**Medium** (CVSS 3.1: 6.5)

`CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N`

- **Attack Vector:** Network — exploitable via Livewire POST to `/livewire/update`
- **Attack Complexity:** Low — Activity IDs are sequential auto-incrementing integers; attacker enumerates 1, 2, 3, ...
- **Privileges Required:** Low — any authenticated user on any page containing the ActivityMonitor component
- **User Interaction:** None
- **Scope:** Unchanged — impact stays within Coolify's data scope
- **Confidentiality Impact:** High — command outputs from remote SSH processes are fully disclosed, including potential secrets, environment variables, configuration files, and infrastructure topology
- **Integrity Impact:** None — read-only disclosure
- **Availability Impact:** None

## Affected Component
- `app/Livewire/ActivityMonitor.php` — `hydrateActivity()` (line 50-59), `updatedActivityId()` (line 61-68)

## CWE
- **CWE-639**: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key (IDOR)
- **CWE-862**: Missing Authorization

## Description

### Unlocked public property enables client-side ID manipulation

In Livewire 3, public properties without the `#[Locked]` attribute can be freely modified by the client through the Livewire update mechanism. The `$activityId` property is public and not locked:

```php
// app/Livewire/ActivityMonitor.php:13
public $activityId = null;  // NOT #[Locked] — client can change this
```

When the client changes `$activityId`, Livewire automatically calls the `updatedActivityId()` lifecycle hook, which triggers `hydrateActivity()`:

```php
// app/Livewire/ActivityMonitor.php:61-68
public function updatedActivityId($value)
{
    if ($value) {
        $this->hydrateActivity();
        $this->isPollingActive = true;
        self::$eventDispatched = false;
    }
}

// app/Livewire/ActivityMonitor.php:50-59
public function hydrateActivity()
{
    if ($this->activityId === null) {
        $this->activity = null;
        return;
    }

    $this->activity = Activity::find($this->activityId);  // ← global lookup, no team check
}
```

### No defense at any layer

The Spatie `Activity` model (`Spatie\Activitylog\Models\Activity`) is used directly without a custom subclass. It has:
- No global scope restricting records by team
- No `team_id` column or team-based filtering
- Auto-incrementing integer primary keys (trivially enumerable)

The `ActivityMonitor` component has:
- No `#[Locked]` on `$activityId`
- No `$this->authorize()` call
- No team ownership check
- No middleware-level protection

### What data is exposed

The Blade template renders the full decoded output of SSH commands:

```php
// resources/views/livewire/activity-monitor.blade.php:55
<pre class="font-mono whitespace-pre-wrap">{{ RunRemoteProcess::decodeOutput($activity) }}</pre>
```

`decodeOutput()` extracts and concatenates all command outputs stored in the Activity's `description` field:

```php
// app/Actions/CoolifyTask/RunRemoteProcess.php:54-74
public static function decodeOutput(?Activity $activity = null): string
{
    $decoded = json_decode(data_get($activity, 'description'), associative: true, ...);
    return collect($decoded)
        ->sortBy(fn ($i) => $i['order'])
        ->map(fn ($i) => $i['output'])
        ->implode('');
}
```

Activity records are created by `remote_process()` and store the stdout/stderr of SSH commands executed on managed servers. These commands include:
- Docker container inspection and management
- Server setup and prerequisite installation
- Proxy (Traefik/Caddy) configuration
- Package updates and security patches
- Database operations and imports
- Cloudflare tunnel configuration
- Service start/stop/restart outputs

### Authorization exists at creation time but not at read time

The `remote_process()` helper (`bootstrap/helpers/remoteProcess.php:38-43`) does verify team membership when **creating** an activity:

```php
if (Auth::check()) {
    $teams = Auth::user()->teams->pluck('id');
    if (! $teams->contains($server->team_id) && ! $teams->contains(0)) {
        throw new \Exception('User is not part of the team that owns this server');
    }
}
```

But the ActivityMonitor component performs **no check when reading** — it blindly loads any Activity ID provided by the client.

### Execution chain

1. **Entry point**: Attacker authenticates and navigates to any page containing `<livewire:activity-monitor />` (9+ pages: server management, database import, service configuration, settings, boarding, etc.)

2. **Property manipulation**: Attacker intercepts the Livewire POST to `/livewire/update` and modifies the component state to set `activityId` to an arbitrary integer (e.g., 1).

3. **Lifecycle hook**: Livewire calls `updatedActivityId(1)` → `hydrateActivity()` → `Activity::find(1)`.

4. **Global lookup**: The Spatie Activity model returns the record regardless of which team created it.

5. **Rendering**: The Blade template calls `RunRemoteProcess::decodeOutput($activity)` and renders the full command output in the response.

6. **Enumeration**: Attacker iterates through Activity IDs (1, 2, 3, ...) to read every activity record in the system, including all command outputs from all teams.

## Proof of Concept

```
1. Login as User A (Team A).
2. Navigate to any page with ActivityMonitor (e.g., /server/{uuid} which has
   the server navbar slide-over with <livewire:activity-monitor>).
3. Open browser DevTools → Network tab.
4. Trigger any action that activates the activity monitor (e.g., validate server).
5. Find the POST to /livewire/update for the ActivityMonitor component.
6. Replay the request, but modify the component snapshot to set activityId to 1
   (or any sequential integer).
7. The response HTML contains the full decoded output of that activity —
   regardless of which team created it.
8. Repeat with activityId = 2, 3, 4, ... to enumerate all activities.
```

## Impact

- **Cross-tenant command output disclosure**: Attacker reads the full stdout/stderr of SSH commands executed on any team's servers
- **Secret exposure**: Command outputs may contain environment variables, database connection strings, API keys, private keys, and other secrets processed during server setup, deployments, and configuration
- **Infrastructure reconnaissance**: Server IPs, Docker configurations, proxy settings, service topologies, and package versions are exposed
- **Full enumeration**: Auto-incrementing integer IDs allow trivial enumeration of every activity record in the database
- **Persistent access**: Activity records are not deleted — historical command outputs remain accessible indefinitely

## Recommended Remediation

### Option 1: Lock the property and add team authorization (preferred)

```php
use Livewire\Attributes\Locked;

class ActivityMonitor extends Component
{
    #[Locked]
    public $activityId = null;

    public function hydrateActivity()
    {
        if ($this->activityId === null) {
            $this->activity = null;
            return;
        }

        $activity = Activity::find($this->activityId);

        // Verify the activity belongs to the current user's team
        if ($activity) {
            $teamId = currentTeam()?->id;
            $activityTeamId = data_get($activity, 'properties.team_id');
            $serverUuid = data_get($activity, 'properties.server_uuid');

            if ($activityTeamId && $activityTeamId !== $teamId) {
                $this->activity = null;
                return;
            }

            // Fallback: check via server ownership
            if ($serverUuid) {
                $server = \App\Models\Server::where('uuid', $serverUuid)->first();
                if ($server && $server->team_id !== $teamId) {
                    $this->activity = null;
                    return;
                }
            }
        }

        $this->activity = $activity;
    }
}
```

Adding `#[Locked]` prevents client-side property manipulation entirely. The authorization check provides defense-in-depth for cases where the property is set via server-side events (`newMonitorActivity`).

### Option 2: Store team_id on Activity records and scope queries

```php
// When creating activities, store team_id in properties:
$this->activity = activity()
    ->withProperties(array_merge($properties, ['team_id' => currentTeam()?->id]))
    ->log('[]');

// When reading, scope by team:
$this->activity = Activity::where('id', $this->activityId)
    ->where('properties->team_id', currentTeam()?->id)
    ->first();
```

This provides database-level enforcement and works even if the `#[Locked]` attribute is accidentally removed.

